# ostree -- flatpak's entire storage and transport layer.
#
# Every flatpak install is an ostree pull: content-addressed objects, hardlinked
# checkouts, and a GPG-signed summary. flatpak takes ostree-1 as a hard
# dependency with no option guard.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
SRC_URL=https://github.com/ostreedev/ostree/releases/download/v2025.4/libostree-2025.4.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=014d12d19ea664fae53fe147d3d2c0b7465a3c37e099c87d289df908adeb5669
SRC_DIR=libostree-2025.4

# THE HTTP BACKEND IS THE ONE REAL DECISION IN THIS RECIPE, AND IT IS SETTLED BY
# A LINE IN FLATPAK RATHER THAN BY A PREFERENCE ABOUT HTTP CLIENTS.
#
# flatpak's meson.build has, with no option guard:
#     libcurl_dep = dependency('libcurl', version : '>=7.29.0')
# So CURL IS LINKED INTO THIS STACK WHATEVER ostree CHOOSES. Picking libsoup here
# would not replace curl, it would ADD to it -- and the same flatpak process
# would then carry two HTTP clients with two TLS configurations, two proxy
# implementations, two redirect policies and two sets of CVEs, in the process
# that verifies and unpacks a signed repository. Two stacks disagreeing about a
# proxy variable or certificate verification produce a failure that works for one
# component and not the other in the same binary, which neither component's code
# explains.
#
# That is why this is not "curl is better than libsoup". The question is not
# which client is better -- that argument is unanswerable and gets reopened
# every year -- but HOW MANY HTTP CLIENTS ONE PROCESS SHOULD HAVE, which has an
# obvious answer.
#
# libsoup3 arrives later regardless: gnome-software requires it, so libpsl,
# nghttp2, sqlite3 and libsoup3 are all coming. That does not reopen this. Two
# clients in TWO PROCESSES is ordinary -- a UI fetching catalogue metadata is a
# different problem from a daemon verifying a signed repository. Two in ONE
# process is the thing being avoided.
#
# ALL THREE DIRECTIONS ARE PINNED, and --without-soup is the load-bearing one:
#     --with-curl     [default=NO]   must be asked for
#     --with-soup     [default=YES]  libsoup2 is ON unless refused
#     --with-soup3    [default=no]
# With libsoup2 absent from the tree today, --without-soup looks decorative and
# is not: it is the flag that stops the choice from being decided by whatever
# happens to be in the seed on some future day. Same shape as libseccomp's
# --disable-static.
#
# --without-composefs IS REQUIRED TODAY, AND IT IS THE FIRST DEFAULT IN THIS
# WORKSTREAM THAT FAILS LOUDLY RATHER THAN DEGRADING QUIETLY. composefs defaults
# to yes and configure ends with
#     as_fn_error "composefs is enabled but could not be found"
# so a missing composefs STOPS THE BUILD instead of silently removing a feature.
# Worth recording as the contrast case: every other default this workstream has
# had to name was dangerous precisely because it did not do this.
#
# --without-selinux: no SELinux, AppArmor or Smack in this distribution. The
# kernel does carry an LSM stack -- CONFIG_LSM is landlock, lockdown, yama,
# loadpin, safesetid, ipe, bpf -- so "no LSM" would be wrong; what is absent is
# specifically the label-based ones ostree integrates with.
#
# --with-crypto=openssl rather than the glib default. openssl is already in this
# stack unavoidably (curl links it, libarchive links it), so this adds no
# dependency, and it is what enables ed25519 and SPKI signature verification
# alongside GPG. Flathub signs with GPG today; ostree signing is moving toward
# ed25519, and having the verifier able to check both costs nothing here.
#
# --disable-http2: ostree's http2 support comes through libcurl, and Duct's curl
# is built without nghttp2 because nghttp2 is not packaged. Naming it means the
# absence is a decision rather than a silent consequence of another recipe's
# dependency list -- and it will need revisiting when nghttp2 lands for libsoup3.
#
# --without-libsystemd, --without-avahi: neither is packaged, and both would
# otherwise be auto-detected.
#
# --enable-introspection=no: gobject-introspection IS packaged, so this would
# otherwise resolve to yes and build GIR data nothing here consumes. flatpak and
# gnome-software both use ostree's C API.
#
# --enable-man=no: needs xsltproc and the docbook stylesheets, neither packaged.
# At its default of auto it would silently resolve to no, which is the right
# outcome for the wrong reason.
#
# --disable-installed-tests: the payload class again -- test binaries installed
# on every system rather than a build-time cost.
#
# KEPT ON DELIBERATELY:
#   --with-libarchive   libarchive is packaged and flatpak wants ostree able to
#                       import from tar streams.
#   rofiles-fuse        default yes and needs fuse3, which flatpak requires
#                       anyway for revokefs -- so this adds no new gate.
# --sysconfdir=/etc. THE GENERIC build.sh PASSES ONLY --prefix=/usr, AND
# AUTOTOOLS DOES NOT SPECIAL-CASE A /usr PREFIX THE WAY MESON DOES -- meson
# maps sysconfdir to /etc when prefix is /usr, autoconf makes it $prefix/etc.
# So without this every file ostree installs under sysconfdir lands in
# /usr/etc, which nothing reads.
#
# THIS WAS NOT FOUND BY READING. install.sh's absolute-symlink assertion
# fired on its first execution and named /usr/etc/grub.d/15_ostree -- I had
# removed $DESTDIR/etc/grub.d/15_ostree, which never existed. The assertion
# caught both the link I missed and the reason the path was not where I
# expected. attr, eudev and shadow pass this flag for the same reason.
CONFIGURE_ARGS="--disable-static --sysconfdir=/etc \
--with-curl --without-soup --without-soup3 \
--with-gpgme --with-crypto=openssl --with-libarchive \
--without-composefs --without-selinux --without-libsystemd --without-avahi \
--disable-http2 --enable-introspection=no --enable-man=no \
--disable-installed-tests"
